WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.080
If a glial cell stopped burning fuel in its mitochondria, would the axon it wraps

2
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.080
starve?

3
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:10.593
Start with a puzzle.

4
00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:17.573
Mice lacking the myelin protein PLP, or the enzyme CNP, made close to normal myelin.

5
00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:22.083
Yet as they aged, their axons swelled and degenerated.

6
00:00:22.843 --> 00:00:26.003
The glial cell seems to give the axon more than insulation.

7
00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:28.743
Fuel is one candidate.

8
00:00:29.703 --> 00:00:31.983
Glycolysis turns glucose into pyruvate.

9
00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:35.843
Pyruvate can enter the mitochondrion, for far more ATP.

10
00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:39.917
Or it can become lactate.

11
00:00:40.557 --> 00:00:46.477
That turns NADH back into NAD+, +, so glycolysis keeps running without mitochondria.

12
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:49.657
Another cell can turn lactate back and burn it.

13
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.810
A cell that stops at lactate keeps only 2 ATP.

14
00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.670
Its lactates carry more than nine-tenths of what the glucose could yield.

15
00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:05.423
Now the test. One laboratory deleted the gene Cox10 in myelinating cells.

16
00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:09.343
Without it, they can make ATP only by glycolysis.

17
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:15.327
If oligodendrocytes must respire to keep axons healthy, what should happen in the

18
00:01:15.327 --> 00:01:17.067
adult brain over the following months?

19
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.710
If they had to respire, myelin and axons should fail.

20
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.530
Instead, the oligodendrocytes survived.

21
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.470
Myelin and axons were largely preserved for months.

22
00:01:37.587 --> 00:01:41.187
Oligodendrocytes that have made their myelin can live on glycolysis alone.

23
00:01:42.187 --> 00:01:46.087
Pyruvate and lactate they cannot burn must leave or be left over.

24
00:01:47.027 --> 00:01:50.467
Leaving would fit glia feeding axons, but this does not show it.

25
00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:52.790
Peripheral nerves differed.

26
00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:57.710
Schwann cells without Cox10 survived but failed to myelinate properly.

27
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.590
The mice developed a severe neuropathy.

28
00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:04.843
Those cells were still building myelin, a costly stage.

29
00:02:05.623 --> 00:02:08.463
They survived, but needed respiration to myelinate.

30
00:02:09.323 --> 00:02:11.403
The stage may explain part of the difference.

31
00:02:12.417 --> 00:02:18.077
One more clue. A scan that measures brain chemicals found extra lactate in the

32
00:02:18.077 --> 00:02:20.097
mutants, but only under anaesthesia.

33
00:02:21.197 --> 00:02:24.797
Within an hour after the anaesthetic ended, it fell below detection.

34
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.940
Why would it show up only under anaesthesia?

35
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.140
One reading: glycolytic glia make more lactate than they burn.

36
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.420
Awake, something uses it as fast as it is made.

37
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.900
Under anaesthesia, that use slows.

38
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:47.613
The axons are an obvious candidate user.

39
00:02:48.213 --> 00:02:51.153
But the experiment never traced where the lactate went.

40
00:02:52.117 --> 00:02:56.837
So switch off the glial cell's respiration, and the axon in the brain is spared,

41
00:02:56.837 --> 00:02:58.097
over the months studied.
